27. Blind verification

Blind aliases are used where:
- true identity is unnecessary;
- blindness does not interfere with the tested proposition;
- sequence or source identity could bias the reviewer.

Applicable cases include:
- Stage 12 run review;
- cross-run review;
- Stage 23 meaning review;
- Stage 24 adversarial review.

Blind aliases are not used when true identity is itself part of the requirement.

The application:
- generates aliases;
- keeps mappings stable for the prompt or package;
- stores mappings durably;
- hides mappings from reviewers;
- binds mappings to prompt identity;
- remaps returned records to canonical IDs;
- rejects reviewer attempts to provide or alter mappings.

True RUN_ID is withheld unless the test requires it.

⸻

28. Prompt context isolation

The actual selected context must match the prompt's stated restrictions.
A warning not to use information is insufficient if the application embeds that information.

28.1 Stage 11 may include
- frozen candidate;
- current reserved run ID;
- current context identity or alias;
- authorized project inputs;
- tool configuration.

It excludes:
- every other run output;
- verification results;
- comparison findings;
- defects discovered after candidate freeze;
- RCA;
- corrections;
- reviewer feedback.

28.2 Stage 12 may include
- exact target run or blind alias;
- target requirement;
- target test;
- required artifacts;
- relevant governing evidence.

It excludes:
- generator self-evaluation;
- other verifier determinations;
- Stage 13 comparison;
- RCA;
- correction proposals.

28.3 Stage 23 may include
- actual product;
- governing requirement;
- applicable source evidence;
- applicable meaning test or rubric.

It excludes:
- generator correctness claims;
- deterministic pass status when unnecessary;
- adversarial findings;
- prior meaning-review conclusions.

28.4 Stage 24 may include
- actual product;
- applicable requirements;
- authorized regressions;
- authorized failure patterns.

It excludes:
- generator reasoning;
- generator self-evaluation;
- conclusions telling the reviewer what to find;
- prior reviewer conclusions unless an explicit attack requires them.

⸻

29. Evidence sufficiency

workflow-engine.js provides:
evaluateEvidenceSufficiency(project, {
  requirement,
  test,
  result,
  executionPlan
})

It returns:
sufficient
reasons
requiredEvidenceClasses
presentEvidenceIds
currentArtifactIds

29.1 Byte identity

Requires:
- application-computed current hashes;
- current canonical artifact identities;
- actual verified bytes;
- correct current-scope relationship.

Agent prose alone is insufficient.

29.2 Programmatic test

Requires:
- registered runtime or declared external capability;
- execution result;
- exact input identity;
- evidence record;
- required returned artifact;
- current test-spec identity.

29.3 Meaning review

Requires:
- requirement identity;
- product location;
- observed meaning;
- evidence-based comparison;
- applicable source evidence.

A hash alone is insufficient.

29.4 Human inspection

Requires explicit human-owned observation evidence recorded through the RECORD_HUMAN_OBSERVATION command.
An AI claim that a human inspected something is insufficient.

29.5 External system

Requires evidence attributable to the declared external system or capability.
Generic prose is insufficient.

29.6 Unavailable capability

UNAVAILABLE can never establish satisfaction.

29.7 Artifact evidence

When an artifact is evidence, the evidence record resolves to the canonical artifact.
Mentioning a filename in prose is insufficient.

29.8 Later artifact loss

Historical execution remains preserved.
Current readiness, release, or evidence-chain completeness becomes blocked or invalid if it depends on bytes that are no longer available and verified.

29.9 Gate integration

The shared evidence evaluator is consumed by:
- Stage 07;
- Stage 12;
- Stage 15;
- Stage 19;
- Stage 22;
- Stage 23;
- Stage 24;
- Stage 25 where evidence is required;
- Stage 26;
- Stage 27;
- Stage 29.

Evidence rules are not reimplemented independently in every stage.

29.10 Activation-proposition proof

Every conditional activation proposition has either its own current PROOF_OBLIGATION_ID or an explicitly identified activation leaf inside the parent applicability proof contract. It uses the same proof-expression, observation, entailment, evidence-class, allowed-epistemic-basis, freshness, contradiction, independence, and semantic-review rules as every other release-bearing proposition.

An agent proposal alone cannot establish activation TRUE or FALSE and cannot activate or deactivate a requirement. Activation UNKNOWN produces applicability UNKNOWN. A changed activation result invalidates the applicability record, parent proof obligation, tests, results, and all downstream release-bearing state.

